PRD: RCL Daily PWA
1. Product Overview
The Lectionary Daily PWA provides a streamlined, distraction-free environment for liturgical reading. It is designed in a minimalist, professionally designed UI that is calming and simple.  It prioritizes immediate access to the current day's lections (Year A for 2026) while silently hydrating a full, offline-capable Bible in the background.
2. Target User Stories
The Daily Reader: "I want to open the app and see today's readings immediately without having to navigate menus or wait for a large download."
The Commuter/Traveler: "I want to be able to read the full Bible and follow cross-references even when I am on a plane or in an area with poor cellular reception."
The Student: "I want to see the context of an RCL reading by jumping easily from the lectionary view to the full chapter in the Bible."

3. Functional Requirements
3.1 The "Today" View (Primary Landing)
Automatic Resolution: On launch, the app calculates the current date and retrieves the corresponding RCL readings (First Reading, Psalm, Second Reading, Gospel) for the current week.
Liturgical Logic: The app must correctly identify the Liturgical Year (e.g., Year A for 2026).
Immediate Loading: Use a "Just-in-Time" (JIT) model to fetch today's text as a high-priority, small-payload JSON.
3.2 Ability to view different day’s reading
Swiping/Scroll: User should be able to swipe and scroll forward or backward to view the next/previous day’s reading from the main page. (eg, swipe left and tomorrow’s reading will appear). The 7 days (mon - sun) around the current date should be accessible immediately. 
3.3 Full Bible & Navigation
Hamburger Menu: Access to a Book/Chapter picker for the entire Bible.
Internal Cross-References: All references within the RCL text (and eventually the Bible) must be interactive. Clicking a reference (e.g., Genesis 1:1) jumps the user to that chapter.
Reading Progress: The app should remember the user's last-read position in the full Bible using localStorage. Reloading the page, however, will lead to the next day’s reading. 
Bible Reading: The app should allow user to progress through the bible chapter by chapter through scrolling and swiping up on mobile. On the desktop, to advance to a new chapter can contain a button UI. 
Search: Users should be able to search within the bible for different references. 
Calendar: Allows user to select a date from a calendar ui and then navigate to any day’s selected reading. 
3.4 Offline Capabilities
Service Worker: Must proxy all requests. If the network is unavailable, it must serve the App Shell and Bible data from local storage.
Background Hydration: After the "Today" view is loaded, the app silently downloads the remaining 5MB of Bible data in the background and saves it to IndexedDB.

4. User Interface (UI) & Experience (UX)
Reader-First Design: High-contrast serif typography optimized for long-form reading.
Offline Status: A subtle indicator (e.g., a green checkmark) appears in the sidebar once the full Bible hydration is complete.
Responsive Layout: Mobile-first, but scales gracefully for tablet and desktop "sidebar" use.
Dark Mode: Dark Mode should be available as a toggle hidden in the hamburger menu. 
The Sidebar: Contains "Today's Lectionary," "Search.", “Calendar” and “Bible” as the primary options. 
Full Bible: Full bible should contain navigation that allows the user to browse via book > then chapter > then verse. 

5. Technical Addendum
5.1 Data Architecture
To ensure high performance and "jump-to" capabilities, we will use a Universal Verse ID (UVID) system.
Bible Schema (IndexedDB): | Field | Type | Description | | :--- | :--- | :--- | | uvid | Integer | 1 to 31,102 (e.g., Gen 1:1 = 1). | | ref | String | Standardized slug (e.g., "JHN.3.16"). | | text | String | The verse content. | | meta | JSON | Contains arrays of internal cross-references. |
5.2 The Hydration Flow (JIT Model)
Initial Load: Fetch manifest.json + current_day.json (~50KB).
App Interactive: UI renders "Today's Reading."
Background Sync: * Service Worker initiates fetch('/assets/full_bible.json').
Data is streamed into IndexedDB in chunks to avoid blocking the main UI thread.
Offline Ready: App sets a is_offline_ready flag in localStorage to true.
5.3 RCL Year Calculation Logic
The app uses the following logic to determine the cycle (divisible by 3 = Year A):
JavaScript
function getRCLYear(date = new Date()) {
    const year = date.getFullYear();
    const christmas = new Date(year, 11, 25);
    const adventStart = new Date(year, 11, 25 - (christmas.getDay() || 7) - 21);
    const liturgicalYear = (date >= adventStart) ? year + 1 : year;
    return ['C', 'A', 'B'][liturgicalYear % 3]; 
}

5.4 Cross-Reference Resolution
References are parsed using a regex (e.g., /([1-3]?\s?[A-Z][a-z]+)\s(\d+):(\d+)/).
Match: "John 3:16"
Action: Maps to slug: JHN.3.16.
Query: db.verses.where('ref').equals('JHN.3.16').first().
5.5 Sourcing of biblical Text
Bible version should be Berean Standard Bible (BSB) which is available as part of the public domain. 

6. Future Considerations
Treasury of Scripture Knowledge (TSK): Integrating the 500,000+ TSK references as an optional "Advanced Context" layer.
Multi-Translation Support: Allowing users to download additional versions (NRSV, ESV) which would be stored as separate tables in IndexedDB.
Share Option: Option on the top right hand of the screen to share to common messages or to common social media. 

